```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The eBGP session fails to establish because of an AS number mismatch in
router2's BGP neighbor statement. Router1 runs in AS 1 and correctly
declares router2 as remote-as 2. Router2 runs in AS 2 but declares router1
as remote-as 65001 — a private/documentation ASN that has no relationship
to router1's actual AS.

During the BGP OPEN exchange, each side advertises its own AS number.
Router1 sends AS 1 in its OPEN; router2 expects AS 65001 and immediately
rejects the session with a NOTIFICATION (Bad Peer AS), causing both peers
to fall back to Idle. Because no session forms, neither router installs the
other's prefix (195.11.14.0/24 or 200.1.1.0/24) in its RIB, so pc1 and
pc2 have no routes to each other's subnet despite the inter-AS link itself
being fully operational (193.10.11.1 ↔ 193.10.11.2 are reachable and
interfaces are up).

This was introduced on router2 during the configuration change window
— the `remote-as` value was incorrectly set to 65001 instead of 1.

Root cause: router2's BGP neighbor statement for 193.10.11.1 specifies
`remote-as 65001` instead of the correct `remote-as 1`.

Corrective fix: On router2, enter BGP configuration mode and change the
neighbor declaration:
  `neighbor 193.10.11.1 remote-as 1`
(replacing `remote-as 65001`). The session will transition to Established
within seconds, both prefixes will be exchanged, and pc1↔pc2 reachability
will be restored.
=== END ===
```